packs/claudinite-growth/skills/unattended-agents/SKILL.md:49:- **Give a recurring routine a standing tracking issue only when its output is a *current picture* nothing else holds** — a status roll-up, a measurement table, a triage verdict over live objects. A routine whose output lands **in the repo** already has a record: the commit, the PR and the file it changed, which diff, carry history, and sit where a reader is already looking, none of which an in-place-rewritten issue body does. Where a tracker does earn its place, title it exactly `Claudinite tracker: <Name>`. Find it by that **exact title string, never a fuzzy or partial match** and never a bare number (a number can dangle, and a partial match risks landing on the wrong issue). Log every run that produces a change there — as a **dated comment**, not a sub-issue — so the routine's output accumulates in one reviewable feed, making it easy to audit what it did over time and to tell when the routine itself needs tuning. One issue per routine, not a shared parent. **Create it already closed** if it doesn't exist yet — that is **two calls**, because issue creation always lands the issue open and a `state: closed` argument on the create call is silently ignored: create it, then close it with a follow-up update. And **never open, close, or reopen it again afterward** — its state carries no meaning here (it's a log, not a work item), so every run leaves the state exactly as found and only ever appends. When the routine reports a **current state** rather than a stream of discrete changes (a branch/PR report, a status roll-up), keep that state in the issue **body**, rewritten each run so the newest snapshot is at the top, and use the dated comments as the per-run trail — the reader sees today's picture first, not the oldest one. (Worked examples: `docs/claude/auto-lessons.md` #365, `docs/claude/auto-fallback-coverage.md` #366, `docs/claude/auto-branch-report.md` #399.) **Read that log before authoring anything the routine may have attempted before** — each run re-derives its candidates independently from the same sources, so runs converge on the same item and re-hit the same rejection; make reading the log a precondition of authoring, not a formatting step at the end. **Read the open PRs touching the same paths too, for the same reason**: where the routine's output is review-gated, a finished run's work sits unmerged and the next run — reading only the default branch — cannot see it, so it redoes the work and throws the earlier PR's copy away. An open PR is work already done; when one already answers the question, say so on it and get it reviewed rather than answering it twice.
=== settings names
// WHAT A MEMBER'S SETTINGS FILE IS CALLED — the names alone, and nothing that
// touches a disk to find one.
//
// The names are split out from `settings-file.mjs` because one reader has no disk:
// the dashboard page runs this module in a browser, unbundled, and a single
// `node:` import anywhere in its graph fails the page's first module load
// (#1286). So the two halves live apart — the vocabulary here, the `existsSync`
// probe that picks between the names beside it — and `settings-file.mjs` re-exports
// everything below, which keeps it the one import every disk-side reader needs.
//
// THE RENAME (#1252). The file is `.claudinite-settings.json`. It was
// `.claudinite-checks.json` when checks were the only thing a member declared, and
// it has not been a file of checks for a long time: it carries the pack
// declaration, the scheduler anchor, the installed versions and the delivery
// override. The old name is still READ, everywhere, because a member's file is
// renamed by a migration record that converges on its own schedule — and until
// every member has run it, both names name the same file.
//
// The tolerance is the LEGACY half of a rename with a stated end: the cleanup that
// deletes it is gated on no member still carrying the old name, never on a date
// (#1252 Phase 3).

export const SETTINGS_FILE = '.claudinite-settings.json';

// Retired from what anything WRITES; still read while members carry it.
export const LEGACY_SETTINGS_FILE = '.claudinite-checks.json';

// Both names, newest first — the read order, so a member mid-rename that somehow
// holds both is read from the one the migration wrote. Exported for the cross-repo
// readers, which have no disk to probe and must try the paths in this order over
// the API.
export const SETTINGS_FILES = [SETTINGS_FILE, LEGACY_SETTINGS_FILE];

// Is this path one of the two settings-file names? For the scans that ask whether a
// changed file is the declaration, which must keep answering yes for a member that
// has not been renamed yet.
export const isSettingsFile = (path) => SETTINGS_FILES.includes(String(path ?? '').split('/').pop());
